WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.310
My pupils kept a reading diary for a term.

2
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.870
The pupils who read more often

3
00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.670
scored higher in the reading test.

4
00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.010
Could I predict a new pupil's mark

5
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.330
from their reading hours?

6
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.250
Today: how a straight line can

7
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.430
predict, and when not to trust it.

8
00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.530
A model is a simple rule learned from

9
00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:25.250
data that makes a prediction.

10
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.760
The simplest model is a straight

11
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.360
line through a scatter plot.

12
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.830
Finding that line is called

13
00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.030
linear regression.

14
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.400
Many lines could pass through the dots.

15
00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.510
The best fit line keeps the gaps between

16
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.670
the dots and the line as small as

17
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:42.030
possible.

18
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.390
The computer finds it in a moment.

19
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.900
Here are average marks for pupils

20
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.280
grouped by hours of reading each week.

21
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.090
They climb steadily, about two

22
00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.030
marks for every extra hour.

23
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.490
The line has a slope and a start.

24
00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.590
Here the slope is two marks per hour, and

25
00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.170
the start is eight marks for no reading.

26
00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.710
So the prediction is eight

27
00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.290
plus two times the hours.

28
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:11.770
Careful with lines.

29
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.250
Our pupils read between zero and five

30
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.830
hours, so the line only knows that range.

31
00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.020
And a line shows a pattern, not

32
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.520
a promise for one pupil.

33
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.150
Quick check!

34
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.350
The slope is two marks per hour.

35
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.710
If a pupil reads three more hours a week,

36
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.290
how many more marks does the line predict?

37
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:35.670
Six more marks.

38
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.360
Three hours times two

39
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.020
marks per hour is six.

40
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.060
That is what slope means: the

41
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.160
change for each extra unit.

42
00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.330
In the app, open the scatter lab

43
00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.070
and show the line of best fit.

44
00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.990
Drag one pupil far from the

45
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.430
others and watch the line tilt.

46
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.180
Your turn.

47
00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.410
Our pupils read up to five hours.

48
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.610
Should we trust the line to predict

49
00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.410
the mark for forty hours a week?

50
00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.970
No, forty hours is far outside our data.

51
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.160
Remember: the best fit line is a model,

52
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.600
its slope is the change per unit, and it

53
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.960
only works inside the data you had.

54
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.550
Try it in class tomorrow: plot height

55
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.890
against arm span on the board, and let

56
00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.970
pupils lay a ruler where the line fits

57
00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:27.250
best.

58
00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.810
Ask them to predict one new pupil,

59
00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.790
then measure and compare.
